    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you go into more detail on that last part? You mentioned that Poland/Romania would need less intervention. What is it about the work culture or the \"reliability\" in SE Asia and Africa that makes them necessitate more hands-on management compared to the Eastern European model? Are there specific cultural or behavioral traits we should be prepping our managers for?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Seoutheast Asia and Sub-Saharan Africa require more hands-on management due to differences in cultural and behavioral traits. In SE Asia, such as Vietnam and Thailand, labor laws and collective bargaining agreements have historically favored workers' rights over employers' interests, leading to higher labor turnover rates and expectations for frequent communication, clear task assignments, and on-site supervision to ensure KPIs. Additionally, some Southeast Asian cultures value hierarchy and authority, requiring managers to be direct and assertive with clear expectations.\n\nIn Sub-Saharan Africa, labor markets are often shaped by informal economies, with many workers having multiple jobs, raising questions around job stability, expectations, and work pace. In Africa, particularly in countries like Nigeria, labor cultures tend to be more formal and hierarchy-based, requiring managers to establish clear trust and focus on performance-based rewards to leverage the local workforce.\n\nManagers in Poland and Romania, in contrast, tend to be more approachable, open to dialogue, and willing to collaborate with workers to achieve established goals. As a result, local managers may not require as much hands-on oversight to ensure KPIs, instead focusing on building relationships and driving a culture of ownership and accountability. To be successful in these regions, managers should be prepared to develop therapeutic relationships",
      "source": "target",
